<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Embroidered Uniform Guard</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1368">1368</a>–<a href="/years/1644">1644</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Jinyiwei</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Embroidered Uniform Guard, or Jinyiwei, was the imperial secret police of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Hongwu Emperor</a> in 1368 as his personal bodyguard and formally established in 1382 with about 500 members, who grew to around 14,000 in three years. Taking orders from the emperor alone, it could overrule judicial proceedings and arrest, interrogate, detain without trial and punish anyone. Its duties were curtailed in 1393 after it allegedly abused its authority in the case of the general Lan Yu, in which about 40,000 people were executed, and restored by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a>. It was disbanded when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-zicheng">Li Zicheng</a>'s rebels overthrew the Ming in 1644.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/embroidered-uniform-guard.jpg" alt="Embroidered Uniform Guard" width="330" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jinyiwei.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Jinyiwei, literally the brocade-clothing guard, can be traced back to about 1360, when its men served as personal bodyguards to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Zhu Yuanzhang</a> and defended him in battle against the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-youliang">Chen Youliang</a>. After Zhu founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> and became the Hongwu Emperor, he doubted the loyalty of his subjects and lived in constant fear of rebellion and assassination. One of the guard's first tasks was to help him spy on them. In 1369 it became an imperial military body, and its duties were widened to include inspecting officials at work in the capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The guards answered to the emperor alone and took their orders directly from him. Their power reached nobles and members of the imperial family as well as ordinary officials, and they could arrest, interrogate and punish without due process. Besides policing the court, they gathered military intelligence on the enemy, took part in reconnaissance and, in wartime, served as political commissars with the Ming armies. They wore a distinctive golden-yellow uniform with a tablet on the torso, and robes known as the python robe and the flying fish robe, and carried a sabre called the embroidered spring knife. Their headquarters lay west of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian">Tian</a>'anmen Square, on the ground now occupied by the Great Hall of the People.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a> restored the guard's powers because he feared discontent among his subjects: he had taken the throne from his nephew in the Jingnan campaign, and the guard strengthened his hold on the court. In the later Ming the Jinyiwei came under the control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-depot">Eastern Depot</a> faction, and as the government sank into corruption it was used again and again to remove political opponents through assassination and prosecution. Among its well-known commanders were Ji Gang and Lu Bing. It was disbanded after 262 years, when the Ming fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-zicheng">Li Zicheng</a>'s rebels in 1644.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Embroidered Uniform Guard” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
